Pyrite IDE extension registry

Scope ownership
---------------
The first successful publication of an ID such as @alice/hello reserves
@alice for that publisher's user ID. The scope is independent of usernames;
it does not need to match the publisher's Discourse username. The owner can
publish more plugins in that scope. Other users, including staff, cannot
publish plugins or releases in it. Existing staff moderation remains available.
Unscoped IDs keep their existing behavior.

Claims are stored independently in pyrite_extension_scopes and survive deletion
of every plugin and package in the scope. Do not delete claims during package,
plugin, or user cleanup, or transfer them when a username changes. There is no
public scope deletion or transfer operation.

Uploading a staged package does not reserve a scope. Claiming happens inside
the publication transaction, so a failed publication leaves no reservation.
A unique scope-name index arbitrates concurrent first publications. Existing
development data is backfilled using the earliest plugin's creator per scope;
other users with older conflicting plugins cannot publish further releases.

Registry API
------------
Metadata endpoints use a literal .json suffix under /pyrite/registry. Package
files keep their .pyrix or .zip suffix. The unreleased /pyrite/api/v1 routes
have been removed; the Discourse frontend and Pyrite IDE must be updated together.

  GET  /pyrite/registry/extensions/:id.json
  GET  /pyrite/registry/extensions/:id/releases.json
  GET  /pyrite/registry/extensions/:id/releases/:version.json
  GET  /pyrite/registry/extensions/:id/releases/:version/package.pyrix
  GET  /pyrite/registry/extensions/:id/releases/:version/package.zip
  GET  /pyrite/registry/advisories.json?releases=hello@1.0.0,@alice/tool@2.0.0
  POST /pyrite/registry/packages.json
  POST /pyrite/registry/extensions.json
  POST /pyrite/registry/extensions/:id/releases.json
  POST /pyrite/registry/extensions/:id/moderation.json
  GET  /pyrite/registry/admin/extensions.json
  GET  /pyrite/registry/admin/extensions/:id.json

An ID may be unscoped or scoped (@alice/tool), including dots. The scope
separator may also be encoded as %2F. Versions use canonical semantic versions,
including prereleases. The suffix is matched separately from these values.
The releases list follows descending semantic-version order and applies the
same visibility rules as individual releases.

Responses use snake_case and semantic roots: extension, release, releases,
package (staged upload), extensions (admin list), and advisories. Extension
objects expose their public string ID as id; references from releases and
advisories use extension_id. Database field names are not the API contract.
Publishing returns an extension or release under the same root as reading it.
Errors retain Discourse's HTTP statuses and errors/error_type response format.
The admin list retains its meta.total/meta.page_size and page/q/flagged/level
query parameters. The existing publishing and moderation request fields are
unchanged.

A release response looks like this (checksum abbreviated for illustration):

  {
    "release": {
      "extension_id": "@alice/tool",
      "version": "1.0.0",
      "manifest": {
        "id": "@alice/tool",
        "name": "Tool",
        "version": "1.0.0",
        "manifest_version": 2,
        "type": "service",
        "platforms": ["linux"],
        "permissions": []
      },
      "artifact": { "sha256": "<64 lowercase hex characters>", "size": 1234 },
      "changelog": null,
      "published_at": "2026-11-08T12:00:00Z",
      "installable": true,
      "moderation": {
        "level": "none", "severity": null, "message": null, "scope": "release"
      }
    }
  }

The IDE derives the package path from the requested registry, ID, and version.
There is no download_url field; metadata never selects a different download
host. The manifest is the published version's manifest, while plugin-wide
presentation fields (summary, repository, owner, topic) belong to extension.

Admin interface
---------------
The staff registry page is /admin/plugins/discourse-pyrite-ide-extensions/registry.
It uses Discourse's admin plugin configuration route and sidebar registration,
with the existing staff-only backend checks. The previous /pyrite/admin page
is removed. Authors still use /pyrite/manage/:id to publish new releases and
manage their own plugins; installation and first publication keep their
existing public page routes.

Development
-----------
Requires the semantic gem (installed on boot through the plugin gem
mechanism). Run the specs with:

  LOAD_PLUGINS=1 bin/rspec plugins/discourse-pyrite-ide-extensions/spec
  LOAD_PLUGINS=1 bin/qunit --standalone --target discourse-pyrite-ide-extensions

